  "source": "NCRB 'Crime in India 2022', Table 1.2 (IPC Crimes, Crime Head-wise) and Table 1.3 (SLL Crimes, Crime Head-wise). Case counts are the published all-India 2022 figures, taken at leaf level so no offence is double counted.",
  "notes": {
    "cases2022": "All-India cases registered in 2022 for this crime head. Used as the national share weight.",
    "group": "Operational grouping used as CrimeHead in the application. Every head belongs to exactly one group, so group totals sum to the national total without double counting. NCRB's own chapter grouping splits some road-safety and women-related offences across chapters; this grouping keeps each offence in one operationally meaningful place while preserving every published head count exactly.",
    "law": "Charging provision. Where the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita 2023 replaced an IPC section, both are shown, since FIRs registered across this period cite either.",
    "gravity": "Heinous / Economic / Non-Heinous, following the classification used in state crime-record practice.",
    "dryStateOnly": "Set on the state Prohibition Act, which only exists in states with total or partial prohibition. Without this, prohibition cases would be generated in states that have no such law.",
    "prohibitionShareByState": "Share of that state's total cognizable cases registered under its Prohibition Act. Gujarat and Bihar dominate the national figure — prohibition enforcement is the single largest crime head in both, which is a large part of why Gujarat's headline crime rate (806 per lakh) sits so far above the national 448. These shares are a modelled allocation of NCRB's verified national total of 7,04,281 prohibition cases across the states that have such a law; they reproduce 99% of that total.",
    "caveat": "2022 is the most recent year for which NCRB has published the full crime-head-wise table. Head shares are applied to 2023 state totals, which is why the generated national mix tracks 2022 proportions while state volumes track 2023 counts.",
    "coverage": "The 186 enumerated heads sum to 54,56,317 of the 58,24,946 cognizable cases NCRB recorded in 2022 (93.7%). The remainder sits in heads NCRB publishes only at aggregate level. Shares are normalised across the enumerated heads, so the generated mix reflects the real relative proportions of everything listed here."
  },
